+++ b/ideas/high_instruction_scheduling.txt
@@ -0,0 +1,64 @@
+Instruction Scheduling for Raptor Lake (Port/Resource-Aware)
+=============================================================
+Priority: HIGH
+
+Problem
+-------
+LCCC emits straight-line machine code in IR order. There is no instruction
+scheduler, so latency-bound chains stall even when independent work exists to
+fill the gaps. This shows up as the ~1.1–1.3× gap on FP/latency-bound kernels
+and in the PGO benchmarks' slower-than-ideal loops.
+
+What GCC/ICC/LLVM do
+--------------------
+- Model each instruction's latency, throughput, and which execution ports it
+  uses (e.g. `mul`/`imul` latency ~3 on a p1 port, `div` ~20+, `fma` on p0/p1,
+  loads on p2/p3).
+- Schedule to (a) shorten the critical path and (b) balance port pressure, so
+  the pipeline never starves a single execution unit.
+
+Raptor Lake specifics (Intel Optimization Manual)
+-------------------------------------------------
+- 6 P-cores (i7-14700KF) with 4 ALU ports (p0,p1,p5,p6) + 2 store ports (p4,
+  p237) + 2 load ports (p2,p3).
+- Integer `add`/`lea`: ~1/cycle, 4 ports. `imul`: ~3 latency, 1 port.
+- FP `add`/`mul`: ~4 latency, 2 ports; `fma`: 4 latency; `div` ~11–13 latency.
+- Memory: load latency ~5; dependent loads chain at ~1/cycle on the load ports.
+- Branch: a 16-byte aligned macro-fused `cmp+jcc` predicts in the hot loop.
+
+What to build
+-------------
+Phase 1: **latency-scheduling within a basic block** (list scheduling).
+  - Build a small per-instruction model keyed by the IR op and type
+    (mov/lea/alu/imul/idiv/load/store/div/cmov).
+  - Compute a DAG from data dependencies (the IR operands already give use-defs).
+  - Emit in priority order: longest critical-path-to-end first, tie-break by
+    port pressure so independent work on different ports interleaves.
+  - Keep it correct-by-construction (never move a load before a store it may
+    alias unless provably safe — start conservative: only schedule within a
+    block, only reorder instructions with no memory-aliasing ambiguity).
+
+Phase 2: **port-pressure balancing** across the hot loop body.
+  - Estimate per-op port usage and alternate dependent FP chains onto p0/p1.
+
+Phase 3: **software pipelining / load hoisting** for counted loops, driven by
+  the PGO trip-count profile (a hot high-trip loop gets the aggressive schedule).
+
+Why it is worth it
+------------------
+- Latency-bound FP and memory kernels are the clearest remaining code-quality
+  gap; the schedule is measurable with the existing A/B harness.
+- A scheduler is largely independent of the rest of the backend, so it can be
+  landed behind an env guard (`LCCC_NO_SCHEDULE`) and bisected safely.
+
+Measurement plan
+----------------
+1. Pick latency-bound kernels (nbody, spectral_norm, matmul, FP chains).
+2. A/B scheduled vs unscheduled with the benchmark runner; confirm no >1%
+  regression on the broad suite.
+3. On the i7-14700KF, confirm with PMU: reduced backend-stall cycles, higher IPC.
+
+Key files
+- src/backend/scheduler.rs (new)
+- src/backend/generation.rs (call scheduler before emission)
+- Intel Optimization Manual: "Instruction Latency and Throughput", port tables.
diff --git a/ideas/high_pgo_loop_versioned_devirt.txt b/ideas/high_pgo_loop_versioned_devirt.txt
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..b6677fff
--- /dev/null
+++ b/ideas/high_pgo_loop_versioned_devirt.txt
@@ -0,0 +1,65 @@
+PGO Loop-Versioned Devirtualization (hoist the guard out of the loop)
+=====================================================================
+Priority: HIGH
+
+Motivation
+----------
+The v11 cost-aware devirtualization fix (single-target ≥95% → don't promote)
+eliminated a +28% regression by simply NOT promoting stable single-target
+sites. But there is a better outcome available: instead of *skipping* the
+promotion, **hoist the target check out of the loop** when the function pointer
+is loop-invariant. Then the per-iteration overhead is one compare + one
+well-predicted branch (executed once), not per call.
+
+The idea
+--------
+For an indirect call inside a loop where the loaded function pointer is
+loop-invariant (dominated by a load that does not change in the loop):
+
+```c
+// before
+for (i...) { s += fp(x[i]); }     // indirect every iteration
+
+// after (loop versioning)
+if (fp == hot_target) {
+    for (i...) { s += hot_target(x[i]); }   // direct, guard hoisted
+} else {
+    for (i...) { s += fp(x[i]); }           // fallback
+}
+```
+
+This is the classic LLVM/GCC loop-versioning transform. It retains the accuracy
+win of a direct call on the hot path *and* the branch-prediction win of a
+guard executed once, so it does not regress even a perfectly-predicted single
+target (the v11 concern disappears: the guard is not in the hot loop).
+
+Why it is hard (and why the cost-aware rule is a correct first step)
+-------------------------------------------------------------------
+- Requires proving the function pointer is **loop-invariant** (a value that
+  reaches the indirect call from outside the loop and is not modified inside).
+- Requires loop versioning infrastructure (clone the loop body, add a header
+  dispatch) — LCCC has the IR pieces (phi handling, block cloning from the
+  inliner/loop unroller) but no general loop-versioning pass yet.
+- The cost-aware ≥95% rule is the *safe subset* that is already integrated; this
+  is its generalization.
+
+Plan
+----
+1. Detect a loop-invariant indirect call site (SCEV/dominance-based).
+2. Clone the loop for the hot target (recorded by the value profiler) and the
+   fallback; add the hoisted guard.
+3. Re-run the optimizer on the direct clone (constant propagation, etc.).
+4. Measure vs plain and vs the current cost-aware rule with the PGO A/B harness.
+5. Fall back to the current skip-rule if the clone cost exceeds the gain.
+
+Measurable success
+------------------
+- Single-target hot loops: beat the "neutral" of v11 (guard hoisted once).
+- Multi-valued dispatch: retain the 1.07× win.
+- Both validated with branch-miss counters on the i7-14700KF.
+
+Key files
+- src/pgo/promote.rs (extend the value-profiler site record with loop context)
+- src/passes/loop_versioning.rs (new) or extend loop_unroll.rs cloning helpers
+- reference: LLVM LoopVersioning / LoopPredication; GCC
+  `-ftree-loop-versioning` (alias) and devirtualization.
